deep.reconstruction:
Context. In 'Analysis situs (première mouture)', June 1986, Grothendieck is axiomatizing a 'structure de contrôle' for a 'topologie combinatoire sans points'. The data on the previous page are:
- a set L of 'lieux' (abstract regions);
- a family of 'figures', which are sets of lieux, built from one-element figures F_x = {{x}};
- a compatibility notion: x and y are compatible when {F_x, F_y} is a figure, and 'disjoint' (C-disjoint) when they are distinct and compatible;
- two subsets A, B of L are C-disjoint when every x in A and every y in B are disjoint.

First paragraph. It restates the condition flagged in the margin of p.30 ('à reformuler cond 4'). Two figures F, G are compatible iff for every member U of F and every member V of G, either U = V or U and V are C-disjoint. In modern terms: put a reflexive symmetric 'compatibility' relation on the lieux. Figures then behave like cliques of pairwise disjoint regions, and two figures are compatible exactly when their union is again such a clique.

Entry of 12 June. Grothendieck reports a negative finding from studying the combinatorics of topological pseudo-disks (D, ∂D).
- The 'lieux' on the interior D − ∂D cannot be modelled just as subsets (contractible components, etc.) of D − ∂D satisfying conditions.
- Each must carry an extra datum: an equivalence datum or an 'order' tied to the boundary ∂D. Presumably this records how the region meets the boundary circle and in which cyclic order, i.e. its combinatorial or isotopy type relative to ∂D.
- Hence the interpretation he had in mind (a control structure = a set L of lieux plus certain sets of parts of L, the 'figures') fails for point-free combinatorial topology.

The next page draws the consequence. A control structure becomes a quadruple C = (M, ≪, R_M, 𝓕):
- M is an abstract set of lieux, no longer subsets of a point set;
- ≪ is a refinement order;
- R_M is a reflexive symmetric compatibility relation;
- 𝓕 is a family of parts of M (figures).
Sub-contexts are subsets stable under refinement. A marginal note asks whether R_M is determined by 𝓕 (x R_M y iff some figure contains both) and conversely whether ≪ and R_M determine 𝓕.

The problem served is a combinatorial, point-free foundation for 'tame' (modérée) topology, the programme announced in the Esquisse.
